  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 1.02 — Isometries of the Plane\n\nIn 1.01 we took the dot product as the carrier of Euclidean structure. Now we ask the question that turns geometry into group theory:\n\n> **Which maps of the plane preserve distance?**\n\nA map $f : \\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R^2$ is an **isometry** if $\\lvert f(x) - f(y)\\rvert = \\lvert x - y\\rvert$ for all $x, y$. No continuity, linearity or smoothness is assumed — only that distances survive.\n\nThe answer turns out to be startlingly rigid, and it arrives in three stages:\n\n1. **Isometries are affine.** Preserving distance forces $f(x) = Ax + b$ with $A$ orthogonal. Nothing wilder can occur — the assumption of linearity is not needed, it is a *consequence*.\n2. **There are exactly four kinds**, and a fixed-point count tells them apart: translations, rotations, reflections and glide reflections.\n3. **Reflections generate everything.** Every plane isometry is a product of at most three reflections.\n\nThe set of all isometries forms a group $E(2)$, the **Euclidean group**, and this is our first worked example of Klein's thesis: a geometry is a group, and the propositions of that geometry are the statements invariant under it. Stage 2 will change the group and watch the geometry change with it.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Distance-preserving forces linear-plus-translation\n\nSuppose $f$ is an isometry. Replace it by $g(x) = f(x) - f(0)$, which is still an isometry and now fixes the origin. We show $g$ preserves the dot product, and that this forces linearity.\n\n**Step 1 — $g$ preserves norms.** $\\lvert g(x)\\rvert = \\lvert g(x) - g(0)\\rvert = \\lvert x - 0\\rvert = \\lvert x\\rvert$.\n\n**Step 2 — $g$ preserves dot products.** This is the **polarisation identity** doing the work:\n\n$$x \\cdot y \\;=\\; \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\lvert x\\rvert^2 + \\lvert y\\rvert^2 - \\lvert x - y\\rvert^2\\bigr).$$\n\nEvery term on the right is preserved by $g$, so the left-hand side is too. The metric determines the inner product — length knows about angle.\n\n**Step 3 — preserving dot products forces linearity.** Let $\\{e_1, e_2\\}$ be an orthonormal basis. Then $\\{g(e_1), g(e_2)\\}$ is orthonormal too (Step 2), hence a basis. For any $x$, the coordinates of $g(x)$ in that basis are $g(x) \\cdot g(e_i) = x \\cdot e_i = x_i$. So $g(x) = \\sum_i x_i\\, g(e_i)$, which is exactly linearity.\n\nTherefore $f(x) = Ax + b$ where $A$ has orthonormal columns: $A^{\\mathsf T}A = I$. Such $A$ form the **orthogonal group** $O(2)$, and $\\det A = \\pm 1$ follows immediately.\n\nLet us confirm polarisation symbolically, since the whole argument hangs on it.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nratprint : false$   /* silence float→rational conversion notices */\n\nvnorm(a) := sqrt(a . a)$\n\n/* Polarisation: the inner product is recoverable from lengths alone.\n   (Named xv, yv rather than x, y — those stay free for plotting later.) */\nxv : [x1, x2]$\nyv : [y1, y2]$\npol : ratsimp( ((xv . xv) + (yv . yv) - (xv - yv) . (xv - yv))/2 - xv . yv )$\nprint(\"polarisation residual = \", pol, \"  (must be 0)\")$\n\n/* A ∈ O(2) exactly when AᵀA = I.  Read off the conditions directly. */\nA : matrix([a, b], [c, d])$\nconds : args( transpose(A) . A - ident(2) )$\neqs : [conds[1][1] = 0, conds[1][2] = 0, conds[2][2] = 0]$\nprint(\"orthogonality conditions: \", eqs)$\n\n/* det A = ±1 is forced, since det(AᵀA) = (det A)² = 1 */\nprint(\"det(AᵀA) = det(A)² = \", factor(determinant(transpose(A) . A)))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 24,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "polarisation residual =  0   (must be 0)\northogonality conditions:  [c^2+a^2-1 = 0,c*d+a*b = 0,d^2+b^2-1 = 0]\ndet(AᵀA) = det(A)² =  (a*d-b*c)^2"
     ]
    }
