   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Solving the orthogonality conditions: $O(2)$ has two pieces\n\nThe three equations say the columns of $A$ are unit vectors and orthogonal to each other. In the plane this is enough to pin $A$ down completely.\n\nThe first column is a unit vector, so it is $(\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta)$ for some $\\theta$. The second must be a unit vector orthogonal to it, and in two dimensions there are exactly **two** such vectors — the perpendicular pointing left, and the perpendicular pointing right:\n\n$$A = \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta & -\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix} = R_\\theta, \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad A = \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta & -\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix} = F_{\\theta/2}.$$\n\nThe first has $\\det = +1$ and is **rotation** by $\\theta$. The second has $\\det = -1$ and is **reflection** in the line through the origin at angle $\\theta/2$.\n\nSo $O(2)$ splits into two pieces, distinguished by the determinant:\n\n- $SO(2) = \\{\\det = +1\\}$, the rotations — a group, and a circle as a space;\n- the coset $\\{\\det = -1\\}$, the reflections — *not* a group, since composing two reflections gives a rotation.\n\nThe determinant is measuring **orientation**: $+1$ preserves handedness, $-1$ reverses it. This is the same sign we met as the sign of the triple product in 1.01, and it is the reason a mirror image of a left hand is a right hand.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Rotation by θ, and reflection in the line at angle α through the origin */\nRot(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), -sin(θ)],\n                 [sin(θ),  cos(θ)])$\n\nRef(α) := matrix([cos(2*α),  sin(2*α)],\n                 [sin(2*α), -cos(2*α)])$\n\n/* trigsimp is too weak to collapse double angles; trigrat canonicalises.\n   Map it over matrices and lists so we can compare them entrywise. */\ntsimp(e) := if matrixp(e) then matrixmap(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else if listp(e) then map(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else trigrat(e)$\n\n/* Column vector.  Note: do NOT call this helper col() — that is a Maxima\n   builtin (column extraction) which invert() calls internally. */\ncvec(v) := transpose(matrix(v))$\n\n/* Both are orthogonal; the determinants separate them */\nprint(\"Rot(θ)ᵀ Rot(θ) = \", tsimp(transpose(Rot(θ)) . Rot(θ)))$\nprint(\"Ref(α)ᵀ Ref(α) = \", tsimp(transpose(Ref(α)) . Ref(α)))$\nprint(\"det Rot(θ) = \", tsimp(determinant(Rot(θ))))$\nprint(\"det Ref(α) = \", tsimp(determinant(Ref(α))))$\n\n/* Ref(α) fixes the line at angle α pointwise and negates its perpendicular:\n   eigenvalues +1 and −1, which is what \"reflection\" means. */\naxis : cvec([cos(α), sin(α)])$\nperp : cvec([-sin(α), cos(α)])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Ref(α)·axis − axis = \", tsimp(Ref(α) . axis - axis), \"  (eigenvalue +1)\")$\nprint(\"Ref(α)·perp + perp = \", tsimp(Ref(α) . perp + perp), \"  (eigenvalue −1)\")$\n\n/* A rotation by θ ≠ 0, π has no real eigenvector at all */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"eigenvalues of Rot(θ) = \", eigenvalues(Rot(θ))[1], \"  = e^{±iθ}\")$",
   "metadata": {},
