   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Composition: two reflections make a rotation\n\nThe group law of $O(2)$ is where the geometry becomes vivid. Three identities do all the work:\n\n$$R_\\theta R_\\varphi = R_{\\theta + \\varphi}, \\qquad F_\\alpha F_\\beta = R_{2(\\alpha - \\beta)}, \\qquad R_\\theta F_\\alpha = F_{\\alpha + \\theta/2}.$$\n\nThe middle one is the important one, and it deserves saying in words:\n\n> **Composing reflections in two lines meeting at angle $\\gamma$ gives a rotation by $2\\gamma$** about the intersection point.\n\nTwo consequences are worth noticing. First, the factor of two explains why a mirror-image of a mirror-image is a rotation rather than the identity — reflecting twice in the *same* line ($\\alpha = \\beta$) gives $R_0 = I$, so every reflection is its own inverse, but reflecting in two different lines does not return you home.\n\nSecond, only the *angle between* the mirrors matters, not their individual positions. So the same rotation can be written as a product of reflections in infinitely many ways — a redundancy we will exploit in §5.\n\nNote also that $R_\\theta F_\\alpha \\ne F_\\alpha R_\\theta$ in general: $O(2)$ is non-abelian, even though its rotation subgroup $SO(2)$ is abelian.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* The three composition laws, verified as matrix identities */\nprint(\"Rot(θ)Rot(φ) − Rot(θ+φ)    = \", tsimp( Rot(θ) . Rot(φ) - Rot(θ + φ) ))$\nprint(\"Ref(α)Ref(β) − Rot(2(α−β)) = \", tsimp( Ref(α) . Ref(β) - Rot(2*(α - β)) ))$\nprint(\"Rot(θ)Ref(α) − Ref(α+θ/2)  = \", tsimp( Rot(θ) . Ref(α) - Ref(α + θ/2) ))$\n\n/* A reflection is an involution: Ref(α)² = I */\nprint(\"Ref(α)²                    = \", tsimp( Ref(α) . Ref(α) ))$\n\n/* Non-commutativity.  Reversing the order sends α + θ/2 to α − θ/2:\n   the mirror ends up in a different place, so O(2) is non-abelian. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Ref(α)Rot(θ) − Ref(α−θ/2)  = \", tsimp( Ref(α) . Rot(θ) - Ref(α - θ/2) ))$\nprint(\"Rot(θ)Ref(α) − Ref(α)Rot(θ) at α=0, θ=π/2:\")$\nprint(\"   \", tsimp( subst([α = 0, θ = π/2], Rot(θ) . Ref(α) - Ref(α) . Rot(θ)) ))$\n\n/* Concrete check of the doubling: mirrors 30° apart compose to a 60° rotation */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Ref(π/3)Ref(π/6) = \", tsimp( Ref(π/3) . Ref(π/6) ),\n      \"   and Rot(π/3) = \", tsimp( Rot(π/3) ))$",
