  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 4. Classification: four kinds, told apart by fixed points\n\nNow put the translation back. Every plane isometry is $f(x) = Ax + b$ with $A \\in O(2)$, and we classify by two questions: does $f$ preserve orientation ($\\det A$), and what does it leave fixed?\n\n| $\\det A$ | Fixed points | Type |\n|---|---|---|\n| $+1$ | everything | identity |\n| $+1$ | none | **translation** |\n| $+1$ | exactly one | **rotation** about that point |\n| $-1$ | a whole line | **reflection** in that line |\n| $-1$ | none | **glide reflection** |\n\nTwo cases need an argument.\n\n**Rotations.** If $\\det A = 1$ and $A \\ne I$, then $A = R_\\theta$ with $\\theta \\not\\equiv 0$, and $I - R_\\theta$ is invertible (its determinant is $2 - 2\\cos\\theta \\neq 0$). So $Ax + b = x$ has the unique solution\n\n$$c = (I - R_\\theta)^{-1} b,$$\n\nand $f$ is rotation by $\\theta$ about $c$ — *not* about the origin. A rotation matrix plus a translation is still just a rotation, relocated.\n\n**Glide reflections.** If $\\det A = -1$ then $A = F_\\alpha$, and $I - F_\\alpha$ is singular — it kills the mirror direction. Split $b$ relative to the mirror axis $u = (\\cos\\alpha, \\sin\\alpha)$:\n\n$$b = b_\\parallel + b_\\perp, \\qquad b_\\parallel = (b \\cdot u)\\,u .$$\n\nThe perpendicular part merely shifts the mirror line by $b_\\perp/2$. The parallel part cannot be absorbed: it slides the plane *along* the mirror. So $f$ is a reflection in the shifted line followed by a translation along it — a **glide reflection** — and it has a fixed point only when $b_\\parallel = 0$.\n\nThe glide is the case people forget. It is the reason the answer is \"four kinds\" and not \"three\", and it is exactly what a line of footprints in the sand exhibits.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Rotations: a rotation matrix plus a translation is a relocated rotation --- */\nprint(\"det(I − Rot(θ)) = \", tsimp(determinant(ident(2) - Rot(θ))),\n      \"  — nonzero unless θ ≡ 0 (mod 2π)\")$\n\n/* Centre of the rotation  x ↦ Rot(θ)x + b  is the unique fixed point */\ncentre(θ, b) := tsimp( invert(ident(2) - Rot(θ)) . cvec(b) )$\n\n/* Example: quarter turn about the origin, then translate by (2,0) */\nc0 : centre(π/2, [2, 0])$\nprint(\"centre of  x ↦ Rot(π/2)x + (2,0)  is \", transpose(c0))$\n\n/* Check that this point is genuinely fixed */\nf_rot(x) := Rot(π/2) . cvec(x) + cvec([2, 0])$\nprint(\"f(1,1) − (1,1) = \", tsimp(f_rot([1, 1]) - cvec([1, 1])), \"  (fixed)\")$\nprint(\"f(0,0) − (0,0) = \", tsimp(f_rot([0, 0]) - cvec([0, 0])), \"  (moved)\")$\n\n/* Symbolic form of the centre for general θ and b */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"general centre = \", transpose(centre(θ, [b1, b2])))$",
