   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 5. Reflections generate everything\n\n> **Theorem.** Every isometry of the plane is a composition of at most three reflections.\n\nThe proof is a three-step algorithm rather than a calculation. An isometry is completely determined by what it does to three non-collinear points $P, Q, R$ — because knowing the distances to three such points pins down a point uniquely. So:\n\n1. If $f(P) \\ne P$, reflect in the perpendicular bisector of the segment $P\\,f(P)$. This maps $f(P)$ back to $P$. Now $P$ is fixed.\n2. If the image of $Q$ is still wrong, reflect in the line through $P$ that is the perpendicular bisector of the remaining discrepancy. Since $P$ is equidistant from both, this line passes through $P$, so $P$ stays fixed. Now $P$ and $Q$ are both fixed.\n3. If $R$ is still wrong, its only remaining option is its mirror image in the line $PQ$ — so one final reflection in $PQ$ finishes.\n\nAt most three reflections, and the count matches the classification exactly:\n\n| Reflections | Determinant | Type |\n|---|---|---|\n| $0$ | $+1$ | identity |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | reflection |\n| $2$ | $+1$ | rotation (mirrors meet) or translation (mirrors parallel) |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | glide reflection |\n\nThe parity of the count *is* the determinant. This is why orientation is a genuinely two-valued invariant: each reflection flips the sign, so an even number of them can never produce a mirror image.\n\n### Homogeneous coordinates\n\nTo compose isometries mechanically it helps to write $x \\mapsto Ax + b$ as a single $3\\times3$ matrix acting on $(x_1, x_2, 1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} A & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}x \\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}Ax + b\\\\ 1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nComposition becomes matrix multiplication and the translation part stops needing special treatment. This trick is not merely convenient — appending a $1$ to the coordinates is the first step towards **projective coordinates**, which Stage 2.02 develops properly.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Isometries as 3×3 homogeneous matrices: x ↦ Ax + b */\niso(A, b) := matrix([A[1][1], A[1][2], b[1]],\n                    [A[2][1], A[2][2], b[2]],\n                    [0,       0,       1   ])$\n\n/* Apply a 2×2 matrix to a coordinate list, staying in list-land */\nact(A, v) := [ tsimp(A[1][1]*v[1] + A[1][2]*v[2]),\n               tsimp(A[2][1]*v[1] + A[2][2]*v[2]) ]$\n\n/* Reflection in the line through point p at angle α.\n   x ↦ Ref(α)(x − p) + p, so the translation part is p − Ref(α)p. */\nreflect_line(α, p) := iso( Ref(α), tsimp(p - act(Ref(α), p)) )$\n\n/* Rotation by θ about the point p */\nrotate_about(θ, p) := iso( Rot(θ), tsimp(p - act(Rot(θ), p)) )$\n\n/* Translation */\ntranslate(b) := iso( ident(2), b )$\n\napply_iso(M, x) := block([r], r : tsimp(M . cvec([x[1], x[2], 1])),\n                          [r[1][1], r[2][1]])$\n\n/* Sanity: reflection in the line y = 1 */\nS : reflect_line(0, [0, 1])$\nprint(\"reflection in y = 1: \", S)$\nprint(\"image of (3, 4) = \", apply_iso(S, [3, 4]), \"  (expect [3, −2])\")$\nprint(\"S² = \", tsimp(S . S), \"  (an involution)\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 18,
