  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "### Footprints: seeing a glide reflection\n\nThe classical picture of a glide reflection is a line of footprints. Each step moves you forward *and* swaps left foot for right — that swap is the reflection, and it is why no single footprint can be brought back onto itself.\n\nBelow we take a deliberately asymmetric shape (so that the mirror flip is visible), apply the glide repeatedly, and plot the orbit. Note that the shape alternates orientation with each application, while $G^2$ — a pure translation — reproduces it unchanged.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* An asymmetric \"foot\": a closed polyline, deliberately not mirror-symmetric.\n   Exact rationals keep the arithmetic exact throughout. */\nfoot : [[0, 1/5], [1/2, 1/5], [7/10, 7/10], [11/20, 1], [3/10, 19/20],\n        [1/4, 11/20], [0, 1/2], [0, 1/5]]$\n\n/* The glide: reflect in y = 0, then slide 7/5 along it */\nGl : tsimp( translate([7/5, 0]) . reflect_line(0, [0, 0]) )$\n\n/* Apply Gl^n to every vertex of the shape */\norbit(M, n) := block([Mn, pts],\n  Mn : ident(3),\n  for i : 1 thru n do Mn : Mn . M,\n  pts : map(lambda([v], apply_iso(Mn, v)), foot),\n  [discrete, map(first, pts), map(second, pts)] )$\n\ncurves : makelist(orbit(Gl, n), n, 0, 5)$\n\nprint(\"G  = \", Gl)$\nprint(\"G² = \", tsimp(Gl . Gl), \"  — pure translation by 14/5, no flip\")$\n\nplot2d(curves,\n       [x, -1/2, 17/2], [y, -13/10, 13/10],\n       [legend, false],\n       [title, \"a glide reflection: slide, flip, slide, flip\"],\n       [same_xy, true])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 25,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "G  =  matrix([1,0,7/5],[0,-1,0],[0,0,1])\nG² =  matrix([1,0,14/5],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])   — pure translation by 14/5, no flip"
     ]
    },
