  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\nStarting from nothing but \"distances are preserved\", we derived a complete classification:\n\n$$f \\text{ an isometry} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; f(x) = Ax + b, \\quad A \\in O(2),$$\n\nand $O(2)$ splits by determinant into rotations ($+1$) and reflections ($-1$). Adding the translation part gives exactly four types, told apart by their fixed-point sets — and every one of them is a product of at most three reflections.\n\nThe chain of implication is worth restating, because each link was a small surprise:\n\n> distance preserved $\\to$ (polarisation) inner product preserved $\\to$ (orthonormal basis) *linear* $\\to$ $A^{\\mathsf T}A = I$ $\\to$ four types.\n\nNo smoothness was assumed anywhere. Rigidity came entirely from the metric.\n\n## What this was really about\n\n$E(2)$ is our first **transformation group**, and Euclidean geometry is precisely the study of what it leaves invariant: lengths, angles, areas, and — for the orientation-preserving subgroup — handedness. Every theorem of school geometry is a statement about $E(2)$-invariants, whether or not it is phrased that way.\n\nThat reframing is the whole content of the **Erlangen program**, and it becomes the organising principle from Stage 2 onwards. The moves available are:\n\n- **Enlarge the group.** Allow $A$ to be any invertible matrix and lengths stop being preserved, but ratios along a line survive: that is *affine* geometry (1.05). Allow projective maps and even parallelism dies, while the cross-ratio survives (2.03).\n- **Change the space.** Keep a notion of \"isometry\" but put it on a sphere or a hyperbolic plane, and the angle sum of a triangle stops being $\\pi$ (2.06–2.07).\n- **Go local.** Let the group act only near each point, and the constant curvature of these homogeneous models becomes the varying curvature of Stage 3 and Stage 4.\n\n## Next\n\n**1.03 — Rotations of Space and Quaternions** repeats this analysis one dimension up. The results are stranger: $SO(3)$ is non-abelian, every rotation still has an axis (Euler's theorem), and the group is double-covered by the unit quaternions — a fact with real consequences for anyone who has met gimbal lock.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## References\n\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Chapter 1 is the closest match to this notebook: §1.3 classifies plane isometries, and Theorem 1.9 is the three-reflections result proved here computationally.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1969) *Introduction to Geometry*. 2nd edn. New York: Wiley. Chapter 3 on transformation geometry.\n- Armstrong, M.A. (1988) *Groups and Symmetry*. New York: Springer. Chapters 1–6, for isometries treated as a group from the start.\n\nThe polarisation argument showing that distance-preserving maps are automatically affine is sometimes called the Mazur–Ulam theorem in its general (normed-space) form; the Euclidean case proved here is elementary and does not need it.\n- ★ Martin, G.E. (1982) *Transformation Geometry: An Introduction to Symmetry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-5680-9. Chapters 1–7 cover this notebook directly.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Pillar four (transformation groups) is the Erlangen reading given here.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}